const COURSES=[
{id:"law101",title:"Introduction to American Law",level:"Pre-Law · Level 1",lessons:[
{id:"sources",title:"What is law, and where does it come from?",time:"18 min",sections:[
["Law is more than a rulebook","Law is a system of enforceable rules. In the United States, rules are created by constitutions, legislatures, agencies exercising delegated authority, and courts. Different rules can apply to the same situation. A lawyer must identify the relevant rule and its source before arguing what should happen."],
["A hierarchy of authority","The U.S. Constitution is the supreme law of the United States. Federal statutes must comply with it, and valid federal law may override conflicting state law under the Supremacy Clause. State constitutions, statutes, regulations, and judicial decisions also matter. The answer depends on the legal question and jurisdiction."],
["Statutes and cases","A statute is a law enacted by a legislature. A regulation is a rule made by an agency under authority delegated by law. A judicial opinion explains a court's decision and may establish binding precedent for lower courts. Not every opinion binds every court."],
["Apply the idea","Imagine a city passes a rule restricting demonstrations in public parks. To analyze it, identify the city rule, state law, and relevant First Amendment doctrine. You cannot decide the issue merely by saying that speech is protected; context, government interests, and applicable legal tests matter."]],
quiz:{question:"A state law conflicts with a valid federal law governing the same issue. Which statement is generally correct?",options:["State law always wins within state borders.","The valid federal law generally takes precedence.","The newest law automatically wins.","A local judge may ignore both laws."],answer:1,why:"The Supremacy Clause generally makes valid federal law controlling when it conflicts with state law. Identifying a real conflict and federal authority still requires analysis."}},
{id:"courts",title:"Federal courts and state courts",time:"20 min",sections:[
["Two overlapping systems","The United States has federal courts and separate court systems in each state. Federal courts have limited subject-matter jurisdiction, meaning they can hear categories of cases authorized by the Constitution and Congress. State courts hear a broad range of civil and criminal disputes."],
["Trial courts and appeals","Trial courts determine facts through evidence and apply the law. Appeals typically review claimed legal or procedural errors from a lower court. An appellate court normally does not hold a new trial just because a party disliked the verdict."],
["Jurisdiction is the starting point","Jurisdiction asks whether a court has legal authority to hear a particular case. Federal-question jurisdiction can exist for claims arising under federal law. Diversity jurisdiction may exist for certain disputes between citizens of different states when other statutory requirements are met."],
["Apply the idea","A disagreement between neighbors about a property fence will usually start in state court, not automatically in federal court. Before deciding, ask which claims are asserted, who the parties are, and whether any special jurisdiction rule applies."]],
quiz:{question:"What is usually the main purpose of an appellate court?",options:["To retry every witness from scratch","To review alleged errors in the lower-court proceedings","To write new legislation","To investigate any crime it chooses"],answer:1,why:"Appellate courts principally review lower-court decisions for errors. Their review is constrained by standards of review, records, and procedural rules."}},
{id:"civilcriminal",title:"Civil law versus criminal law",time:"20 min",sections:[
["Different purposes","Criminal cases are prosecuted by the government for alleged offenses against public law and can lead to penalties such as incarceration or fines. Civil cases generally resolve disputes involving rights, obligations, or injuries, often with remedies such as damages or injunctions."],
["Who brings the case?","In a criminal case, the government is the prosecutor. In a civil case, a plaintiff generally sues a defendant. One event can create both kinds of proceedings, but the legal issues, procedures, and burdens of proof differ."],
["Burdens of proof","To convict a defendant in a criminal case, the prosecution must prove each element beyond a reasonable doubt. In many ordinary civil cases, a plaintiff must prove a claim by a preponderance of the evidence, meaning more likely than not. Some civil claims have different standards."],
["Apply the idea","Suppose someone punches another person. The state may prosecute criminal assault, while the injured person may separately sue for civil battery. Winning or losing one does not automatically determine the other."]],
quiz:{question:"Which burden of proof normally applies to a criminal conviction?",options:["Reasonable suspicion","Preponderance of the evidence","Beyond a reasonable doubt","Any evidence at all"],answer:2,why:"The prosecution must prove every element of a charged crime beyond a reasonable doubt for a conviction."}},
{id:"cases",title:"How to read a judicial opinion",time:"25 min",sections:[
["Start with a case brief","A case brief summarizes the key information in a judicial opinion. Use FIRAC: Facts, Issue, Rule, Application, Conclusion. The goal is to understand why the court reached its result, not simply memorize the outcome."],
["Facts and procedural posture","Ask what happened and how the case reached this court. Procedural posture matters: a motion to dismiss, summary judgment, trial verdict, and appeal ask different questions of the judge."],
["The issue and the holding","The issue is the precise legal question. The holding is the court's resolution of that issue. Distinguish the binding rule and reasoning from other observations, sometimes called dicta. Precedential force depends on jurisdiction, court hierarchy, and whether the decision remains good law."],
["Practice with a hypothetical","A cafe customer slips on spilled water. A court considering summary judgment might ask whether sufficient evidence exists for a reasonable jury to find that the cafe knew or should have known about the hazard. Without knowing the governing state law and factual record, you cannot reliably predict the outcome."]],
quiz:{question:"In a case brief, what does the 'Issue' identify?",options:["The name of the losing lawyer","The precise legal question the court must resolve","Every fact mentioned in testimony","Only the damages awarded"],answer:1,why:"A useful case brief frames the issue as a specific legal question tied to the material facts."}},
{id:"reasoning",title:"Think like a lawyer: IRAC practice",time:"30 min",sections:[
["Learn IRAC","IRAC stands for Issue, Rule, Application, and Conclusion. Law students use this framework to write structured answers. The most important part is usually Application: explaining how the facts do or do not satisfy each element of a legal rule."],
["Separate fact from assumption","Careful legal reasoning distinguishes facts given in the problem from information you do not know. Do not fill factual gaps with confident guesses. Identify what additional evidence would change the analysis."],
["Argue both sides","A strong legal analysis considers plausible arguments on each side. For a disputed rule, examine the text, relevant precedent, factual differences, and possible exceptions. A conclusion should reflect uncertainty when facts or law are unsettled."],
["Hypothetical exercise","A shopper alleges a store caused an injury by leaving merchandise in an aisle. Issue: could the store be negligent? Rule: negligence typically requires duty, breach, causation, and damages, subject to state law. Application: analyze foreseeability, warning, notice, injury, and alternative causes. Conclusion: explain what is supportable and what evidence is missing."]],
quiz:{question:"Which IRAC section explains how the legal rule relates to specific facts?",options:["Issue","Rule","Application","Conclusion"],answer:2,why:"Application connects law to facts, tests each relevant element, and considers competing arguments."}}
]},
{id:"law102",title:"Constitutional Foundations",level:"Pre-Law · Level 1",lessons:[{"id":"law102-structure","title":"The Constitution and three branches","time":"25 min","sections":[["The Constitution is the foundation","The Constitution establishes the structure of the federal government, allocates powers, and limits government action. Its original seven articles describe the framework. Amendments modify the text and protect significant rights. The first three articles concern Congress, the President, and federal courts."],["Who does what?","Article I vests federal legislative power in Congress. Article II vests executive power in the President. Article III establishes the federal judicial power. Each branch has enumerated responsibilities, but none has unlimited authority."],["Checks and balances","Congress can enact bills, the President may veto legislation, and Congress can override some vetoes by a supermajority. Federal courts can determine whether government action is consistent with the Constitution in cases properly before them. The specific procedures and constitutional limits are important."],["Try a hypothetical","Congress passes a law, the President vetoes it, and Congress then votes to override. Identify which branches acted, what powers they exercised, and what additional constitutional conditions must be satisfied. Do not confuse a policy disagreement with a constitutional violation."]],"quiz":{"question":"Which article principally establishes the federal judiciary?","options":["Article I","Article II","Article III","Article VII"],"answer":2,"why":"Article III establishes federal judicial power; Article I concerns Congress and Article II the executive."}},{"id":"law102-federalism","title":"Federalism and the Supremacy Clause","time":"30 min","sections":[["Federal and state authority","Federalism divides governing authority between national and state governments. The national government has powers granted by the Constitution; states retain substantial powers to govern matters within their jurisdictions, subject to constitutional restrictions."],["Supremacy is not unlimited power","Article VI makes the Constitution and federal law made pursuant to it supreme over conflicting state law. But the federal government must have constitutional authority for the law in question. A claimed conflict requires careful analysis."],["The Tenth Amendment","The Tenth Amendment reserves powers not delegated to the United States nor prohibited to states to the states or the people. It does not automatically invalidate any federal rule affecting a traditional state concern."],["Try a hypothetical","A state and a federal regulator issue incompatible requirements for a business. Ask: what federal statute authorizes regulation, is it valid, do the requirements actually conflict, and has Congress displaced state regulation? A complete answer cannot rest on the word federal alone."]],"quiz":{"question":"What is the first issue when someone argues federal law overrides a state rule?","options":["Whether federal law is constitutionally valid and actually preempts the state rule","Whether the governor disagrees with the President","Which rule was written first","Whether the state rule is longer"],"answer":0,"why":"Supremacy and preemption depend on constitutionally valid federal law and the nature of the conflict or congressional preemptive intent."}},{"id":"law102-rights","title":"The Bill of Rights and constitutional claims","time":"30 min","sections":[["Rights and limitations","The first ten amendments are called the Bill of Rights. They address, among other things, speech and religion, criminal procedure protections, and powers reserved to the states or people. Many provisions have nuanced exceptions and tests developed in case law."],["Government action matters","Many federal constitutional rights constrain government conduct rather than every private disagreement. A person removed from a private dinner party usually cannot establish a First Amendment violation solely from that fact. There are complex state-action exceptions and other applicable laws."],["State application","Many Bill of Rights protections apply to state and local governments through the Fourteenth Amendment. This process is commonly called incorporation. Not every constitutional provision applies identically in every context."],["Try a hypothetical","A city denies a permit for a peaceful demonstration based solely on the viewpoint expressed. Identify whether government action is present, what constitutional protection may apply, and what additional facts are needed. Do not assume every permit restriction is unconstitutional."]],"quiz":{"question":"Why might the First Amendment apply differently to a city government and a private social club?","options":["Only cities have rules","The First Amendment ordinarily restrains government action, subject to legal exceptions","Private clubs are federal courts","Free speech never applies in public spaces"],"answer":1,"why":"The First Amendment generally constrains government action. Separate statutes or state laws may regulate private entities."}},{"id":"law102-marbury","title":"Case study: Marbury v. Madison","time":"35 min","sections":[["The historical dispute","Near the end of President John Adams's term, William Marbury was appointed a justice of the peace, but his commission was not delivered. The incoming administration did not deliver it. Marbury asked the Supreme Court for an order compelling delivery."],["The central jurisdiction question","Chief Justice John Marshall's opinion considered whether Marbury had a right, whether law provided a remedy, and whether the Supreme Court could issue the requested writ in that proceeding. The Court held that a statutory grant of original jurisdiction conflicted with the Constitution."],["Judicial review","Marbury v. Madison (1803) became a foundational statement of judicial review: courts deciding cases can decline to give effect to statutes that conflict with the Constitution. The Constitution, not an inconsistent statute, controls."],["Brief the case with IRAC","Issue: could the Supreme Court issue the requested writ in its original jurisdiction? Rule: Congress cannot expand Article III original jurisdiction in a way inconsistent with the Constitution. Application: the Court concluded the statutory authorization exceeded that scope. Conclusion: the Court denied the requested remedy while establishing an influential constitutional principle. Research note: read the original opinion and distinguish the Court's stated reasoning from later interpretations."]],"quiz":{"question":"What constitutional principle is Marbury v. Madison best known for establishing?","options":["Congress must approve every court judgment","Judicial review of unconstitutional government acts","State governors appoint Supreme Court justices","All federal cases start at the Supreme Court"],"answer":1,"why":"Marbury articulated the federal judiciary's power to treat laws that conflict with the Constitution as invalid in cases within its jurisdiction."}}]},
{id:"law103",title:"Legal Research & Citation",level:"Pre-Law · Level 1",lessons:[{"id":"law103-1","title":"Finding law: primary vs secondary sources","time":"25 min","sections":[["Begin with the legal question","Before searching, identify the jurisdiction, relevant facts, area of law, and date. A federal constitutional issue and a Texas landlord-tenant question call for different authorities."],["Primary authority","Constitutions, statutes, administrative regulations, and judicial opinions are primary sources. They state law. Legal encyclopedias, treatises, and law review articles explain law but are secondary sources."],["Build a research trail","Start with a credible overview to learn vocabulary; locate the controlling statute or case; read the actual text; then record jurisdiction, date, and source. Do not treat a search snippet as the rule."],["Worked example","A tenant asks whether a landlord must return a deposit. Research the relevant state's statute, any local rules, and controlling cases; collect the lease and move-out dates before concluding. A generic national article cannot reliably settle a state-specific dispute."]],"quiz":{"question":"Which of these is ordinarily primary legal authority?","options":["A law student's blog","A state statute enacted by the legislature","A textbook chapter","A news summary"],"answer":1,"why":"Statutes are enacted law; commentary can help locate and interpret primary materials but usually does not itself establish a binding legal rule."}},{"id":"law103-2","title":"Court hierarchy and binding precedent","time":"28 min","sections":[["Why jurisdiction matters","A court's reasoning may be helpful without being binding. Identify whether the relevant court is federal or state, which appellate court issued an opinion, and whether it sits above the court deciding the current case."],["Binding versus persuasive authority","A controlling higher court's holding ordinarily binds courts below it on applicable issues. Decisions from other jurisdictions may be persuasive but do not automatically control."],["Holding, dicta, and facts","A holding resolves a question necessary to the outcome. Other commentary may be dicta. Compare materially relevant facts and procedural posture before claiming a case controls."],["Worked example","An appellate court in another state decides a similar contract dispute. You may cite it as persuasive support, but you still must find the controlling law in your own jurisdiction."]],"quiz":{"question":"A federal appellate opinion from a different circuit is generally what kind of authority for a federal district court?","options":["Automatically binding","Persuasive, subject to Supreme Court or local circuit law","A statute","Not legal authority at all"],"answer":1,"why":"Other circuit decisions may be persuasive; Supreme Court and controlling circuit precedent govern relevant federal questions."}},{"id":"law103-3","title":"Searching statutes and opinions","time":"30 min","sections":[["Use trusted collections","Locate statutes on official legislative websites and opinions through court websites or reputable legal databases. Cornell's Legal Information Institute and the Constitution Annotated offer useful starting references."],["Keyword strategies","Search names of legal claims, statutory terms, and synonyms. Record citations as you go. Narrow by jurisdiction and year, and inspect cited statutes and cases."],["Confirm currency","An opinion might have been reversed, limited, or superseded; a statute might have been amended. With free tools you can inspect subsequent history and citing authorities, but comprehensive citator checks may require specialized services."],["Practice path","Suppose you encounter a First Amendment protest question: find the amendment's text, then identify the applicable standards for restrictions in public forums from controlling opinions. Separate legal doctrine from the facts being disputed."]],"quiz":{"question":"Why is checking whether a case remains good law important?","options":["Older opinions never matter","It may have been overruled, limited, or superseded","Only case names need verification","Courts never change legal rules"],"answer":1,"why":"Legal authority may change through later judicial decisions and legislative amendments."}},{"id":"law103-4","title":"Legal citations and ethical source use","time":"30 min","sections":[["Purpose of citation","A citation tells readers where to locate authority and assess how it supports your proposition. In American legal writing, the Bluebook is widely used, although courts and publications may require their own rules."],["An introductory case citation","A case citation commonly includes the party names, reporter volume, reporter abbreviation, first page, and year. Example: Marbury v. Madison, 5 U.S. (1 Cranch) 137 (1803). Exact formatting and pinpoint citation rules depend on context."],["Avoid unsupported claims","Do not invent opinions, citations, quotations, or holdings. Distinguish quotation from paraphrase and disclose when an authority addresses a related rather than identical issue."],["Research log exercise","Create a short log containing the issue, jurisdiction, search terms, source URL, date checked, rule or holding, and uncertainty. This prepares you for serious legal research assignments."]],"quiz":{"question":"Which source should you inspect before quoting a court's holding?","options":["An unsourced social media post","The opinion itself from a reliable reporter or court source","A headline alone","An AI-generated citation without verification"],"answer":1,"why":"Use the underlying authoritative text and verify quotations and citations."}}]},
{id:"law104",title:"Legal Writing & Argument",level:"Pre-Law · Level 1",lessons:[{"id":"law104-1","title":"IRAC: issue, rule, application, conclusion","time":"30 min","sections":[["The structure","IRAC organizes legal writing into Issue, Rule, Application, and Conclusion. A reliable answer names the precise legal question, gives an accurate rule, applies each element to facts, and states a supported conclusion."],["Issue and rule","Frame the issue as a question: Can a customer show negligence when a store knew about a spill? Then identify the duty, breach, causation, and harm requirements under the relevant jurisdiction's law."],["Application carries the analysis","Connect each element to the evidence. Consider facts favoring both sides: notice of the spill, warning signs, the time available for cleanup, and whether the spill caused the injury."],["Sample conclusion","If evidence shows employees knew about a dangerous spill for a substantial period yet did nothing, breach may be easier to establish; but the claim still requires causation and damages, and governing law may differ."]],"quiz":{"question":"In IRAC, what is the purpose of Application?","options":["List legal terms","Connect the elements of the rule to disputed facts and counterarguments","Announce a conclusion without analysis","Replace reliable sources"],"answer":1,"why":"Application is the legal reasoning connecting doctrine to the particular facts."}},{"id":"law104-2","title":"Writing an objective legal memo","time":"35 min","sections":[["Who is the audience?","An objective memo helps a supervising lawyer or client understand likely outcomes and uncertainties. It is not a sales pitch for one preferred conclusion."],["Recommended structure","Use Question Presented, Brief Answer, Facts, Discussion, and Conclusion. In the Discussion, organize analysis by legal elements and explain stronger and weaker arguments."],["Predict carefully","Avoid guarantees when facts are disputed, standards are unsettled, or jurisdictions differ. State missing information and how it could change the outcome."],["Worked memo problem","A customer claims a shop promised a refund but refused it. Identify written terms, purchase date, communications, jurisdiction, and applicable consumer law before predicting a remedy."]],"quiz":{"question":"What best distinguishes an objective legal memo?","options":["It hides opposing authorities","It analyzes favorable and unfavorable evidence honestly","It always advocates the client's preferred result","It omits uncertainties"],"answer":1,"why":"Objective legal analysis acknowledges counterarguments, controlling authority, and factual gaps."}},{"id":"law104-3","title":"Arguments and counterarguments","time":"30 min","sections":[["Build a claim","Begin with a clear assertion, follow with the rule, support it with relevant evidence and authority, and explain the inference."],["Steelman the opposition","Present the strongest plausible opposing reading rather than an easy caricature. Then explain the legal or factual reason you believe one position has more support."],["Authority matters","A persuasive sentence is not necessarily legally sound. Test whether sources are binding, whether the holding fits, and whether relevant exceptions apply."],["Practice both sides","A city requires advance permits for large gatherings. Argue why it could be a neutral time-place-manner regulation and why the particular application could nonetheless burden protected expression. Facts and controlling doctrine determine the result."]],"quiz":{"question":"Why should legal writing discuss the strongest counterargument?","options":["To avoid reaching any conclusion","To test and improve the analysis and disclose litigation risk","Because all positions are equally valid","To replace citations"],"answer":1,"why":"Serious legal analysis evaluates plausible competing arguments before reaching a defensible conclusion."}},{"id":"law104-4","title":"Case briefing and factual precision","time":"35 min","sections":[["Case brief essentials","Record citation, procedural history, material facts, issue, rule, holding, reasoning, and any important separate opinions. Summaries should distinguish what the court decided from what it merely discussed."],["Spot facts that change the rule","When comparing hypotheticals to precedent, identify which changed facts affect a rule's elements or application."],["Explain procedural posture","A court reviewing a dismissal may assume pleaded facts are true under the relevant standard; after trial, the evidentiary record and review standards may differ."],["Practice case brief","Use Marbury v. Madison, 5 U.S. (1 Cranch) 137 (1803). State the question about original jurisdiction, the Court's reasoning concerning judicial review, and why the requested writ was not issued. Verify your brief against the opinion."]],"quiz":{"question":"Which item is essential in a case brief?","options":["The author's personal political views","The court's holding and reasoning tied to the issue","Only the winning party's name","An invented factual background"],"answer":1,"why":"A brief explains the actual issue, holding, and reasoning in context."}}]},
{id:"law105",title:"Everyday Legal Problems",level:"Pre-Law · Level 1",lessons:[{"id":"law105-1","title":"Contracts in everyday life","time":"25 min","sections":[["Core concept","A contract generally involves mutual assent, consideration, and sufficiently definite terms, although details and exceptions vary by jurisdiction. Offers, acceptances, and promises must be evaluated in context."],["Work through a hypothetical","Suppose a contractor offers to repair a roof for a quoted price and the homeowner agrees. Identify the parties, terms, exchanged promises, and evidence of acceptance. Ask whether a writing is required under the applicable law."],["What you should remember","Identify the relevant legal issue, select the governing jurisdiction and rule, apply the rule to known facts, and acknowledge missing evidence. This is educational practice, not individualized legal advice."]],"quiz":{"question":"A signed written document is always necessary for every enforceable contract.","options":["True","False","Only if payment is in cash","Only in federal court"],"answer":1,"why":"Many contracts can be enforceable without signatures, but statutes such as a state's statute of frauds require writings for certain categories."}},{"id":"law105-2","title":"Negligence and civil liability","time":"30 min","sections":[["Core concept","Negligence commonly requires duty, breach, factual and legal causation, and legally recognized harm. The particular duty and defenses depend on state law."],["Work through a hypothetical","A visitor slips in a store after an employee noticed a spill. Analyze notice, reasonable care, causation, and actual injury; also consider what evidence is missing."],["What you should remember","Identify the relevant legal issue, select the governing jurisdiction and rule, apply the rule to known facts, and acknowledge missing evidence. This is educational practice, not individualized legal advice."]],"quiz":{"question":"Which item is generally an element of negligence?","options":["Proof beyond a reasonable doubt","A duty of care","A criminal conviction","A signed warranty"],"answer":1,"why":"Negligence generally requires a duty, breach, causation, and damages; the governing jurisdiction determines the precise rules."}},{"id":"law105-3","title":"Criminal charges and due process","time":"30 min","sections":[["Core concept","Criminal prosecution is brought by government, and guilt must be established beyond a reasonable doubt. A charge is an allegation, not a conviction. Constitutional protections and procedural rules govern searches, counsel, trials, and sentencing."],["Work through a hypothetical","Someone is accused of theft after leaving a store with unpaid merchandise. Ask what mental state the offense requires, what evidence exists, and whether lawful procedures were followed. Avoid deciding guilt from an accusation alone."],["What you should remember","Identify the relevant legal issue, select the governing jurisdiction and rule, apply the rule to known facts, and acknowledge missing evidence. This is educational practice, not individualized legal advice."]],"quiz":{"question":"What does the presumption of innocence mean?","options":["A prosecutor must prove guilt under the applicable standard","Police may never arrest","Every accused person receives damages","The defendant must prove innocence"],"answer":0,"why":"In criminal trials, the prosecution bears the burden of proving all offense elements beyond a reasonable doubt."}},{"id":"law105-4","title":"Housing, consumers, and practical research","time":"30 min","sections":[["Core concept","Tenant rights, consumer protection, debt collection, and small claims procedures depend strongly on location. The first skill is identifying the controlling state and local law and reading actual notices, contracts, and dates."],["Work through a hypothetical","A tenant receives a notice about a deposit deduction. Preserve the lease, photographs, receipts, move-out dates, and notice. Research the applicable statute and deadlines before forming a conclusion."],["What you should remember","Identify the relevant legal issue, select the governing jurisdiction and rule, apply the rule to known facts, and acknowledge missing evidence. This is educational practice, not individualized legal advice."]],"quiz":{"question":"What should you establish first in a landlord-deposit dispute?","options":["The most popular national blog's opinion","The property's jurisdiction and applicable law","The landlord's political views","The renter's preferred remedy"],"answer":1,"why":"Landlord-tenant law and procedures are jurisdiction-specific, making location and governing law essential."}}]},
{id:"law106",title:"Introduction to Legal Ethics",level:"Pre-Law · Level 1",lessons:[{"id":"law106-1","title":"What lawyers owe their clients","time":"25 min","sections":[["Core concept","Professional responsibility rules typically impose duties of competence, diligence, communication, loyalty, and confidentiality. The ABA Model Rules are a model, not automatically the binding rules of every state."],["Work through a hypothetical","A lawyer receives an urgent filing deadline but cannot finish the work alone. Identify the risks and consider competent assistance, communication, and appropriate steps under local professional rules."],["What you should remember","Identify the relevant legal issue, select the governing jurisdiction and rule, apply the rule to known facts, and acknowledge missing evidence. This is educational practice, not individualized legal advice."]],"quiz":{"question":"Which is generally an attorney's professional obligation?","options":["Ignore client inquiries","Act with reasonable diligence","Guarantee every case's outcome","Share client secrets for publicity"],"answer":1,"why":"Diligence is a core professional duty, while guarantees and improper disclosures are not."}},{"id":"law106-2","title":"Confidentiality and privilege","time":"30 min","sections":[["Core concept","Confidentiality rules cover information related to representing a client, subject to jurisdictional exceptions. Attorney-client privilege is a distinct evidentiary protection for certain confidential lawyer-client communications made for legal advice."],["Work through a hypothetical","A client tells a lawyer private facts about an ongoing dispute. Distinguish whether ethical confidentiality applies from whether a particular communication is privileged, and check the rules for exceptions."],["What you should remember","Identify the relevant legal issue, select the governing jurisdiction and rule, apply the rule to known facts, and acknowledge missing evidence. This is educational practice, not individualized legal advice."]],"quiz":{"question":"How do professional confidentiality and attorney-client privilege relate?","options":["They are exactly identical","Confidentiality is an ethical duty; privilege is an evidentiary doctrine","Neither has exceptions","Only privilege applies to lawyers"],"answer":1,"why":"The ethical duty of confidentiality and the evidentiary privilege have different scopes and exceptions."}},{"id":"law106-3","title":"Conflicts of interest","time":"30 min","sections":[["Core concept","Representing clients with opposing or materially conflicting interests may threaten loyalty and independent judgment. Conflict rules vary, and some conflicts can be addressed with informed consent while others cannot."],["Work through a hypothetical","An attorney is asked to represent two business partners in a lawsuit against one another. Identify the direct adversity, evaluate whether representation is permissible, and avoid assuming consent fixes every conflict."],["What you should remember","Identify the relevant legal issue, select the governing jurisdiction and rule, apply the rule to known facts, and acknowledge missing evidence. This is educational practice, not individualized legal advice."]],"quiz":{"question":"What is the best first response to a potential client conflict?","options":["Ignore it until trial","Identify and evaluate the conflict under governing rules","Ask clients not to mention it","Assume consent always resolves it"],"answer":1,"why":"Counsel must evaluate conflicts; some are nonconsentable, and permitted waivers require appropriate conditions."}},{"id":"law106-4","title":"Candor, honesty, and access to justice","time":"30 min","sections":[["Core concept","Lawyers have duties of candor toward tribunals, honesty in dealings, and respect for legal processes. They may advocate vigorously, but cannot knowingly misrepresent material facts or law under applicable rules."],["Work through a hypothetical","A lawyer discovers a case cited in a draft memorandum was fabricated. The lawyer must verify authorities, correct the draft, and follow applicable disclosure obligations rather than filing the false citation."],["What you should remember","Identify the relevant legal issue, select the governing jurisdiction and rule, apply the rule to known facts, and acknowledge missing evidence. This is educational practice, not individualized legal advice."]],"quiz":{"question":"What is the appropriate response to discovering a fabricated case citation?","options":["File it if the argument sounds convincing","Verify and correct the authority before relying on it","Change the case name slightly","Assume citations are optional"],"answer":1,"why":"Accuracy and candor require verifying citations; knowingly presenting false legal authority can violate professional rules."}}]}
];
// Batch 1: additional original case-based foundational lessons across six courses.
const EXPANSION=[["law101","Jurisdiction and venue in a real dispute","A claim can be legally strong yet filed in the wrong court. Subject-matter jurisdiction concerns the court's power to hear a type of dispute; personal jurisdiction concerns power over the parties. Venue concerns the appropriate location among authorized courts.","A buyer in one state orders a product from a company operating in another. Identify the claims, each party's contacts, the forum, and potential federal or state jurisdiction before selecting a court.","Which concept asks whether a court has power over the type of case?",["Subject-matter jurisdiction","Venue alone","Choice of attorney","Jury size"],0,"Subject-matter jurisdiction concerns authority over the category of dispute.","https://www.uscourts.gov/about-federal-courts/court-role-and-structure"],["law101","The timeline of a civil lawsuit","A civil claim ordinarily progresses through pleading, service, responses, discovery, motions, possible settlement, trial, and appeal. Procedures, deadlines and available remedies vary by jurisdiction and case type.","A customer sues a retailer over an injury. Distinguish what must be alleged in a complaint from what evidence must eventually establish. Explain why settlement does not necessarily mean admission of liability.","What is discovery generally used for?",["Exchanging and obtaining relevant evidence under procedural rules","Issuing a criminal sentence","Writing a statute","Selecting appellate judges"],0,"Discovery permits parties to obtain information under procedural limits and judicial supervision.","https://www.uscourts.gov/about-federal-courts/types-cases/civil-cases"],["law102","Equal protection and constitutional classifications","The Fourteenth Amendment's Equal Protection Clause constrains state government classifications. Courts apply different standards of review depending on the classification and interest involved; analysis requires identifying the government action, classification and applicable doctrine.","A city ordinance treats two groups differently. Identify the classification, governmental purpose, and applicable standard before claiming the policy is lawful or unlawful.","Why does the applicable level of scrutiny matter?",["It affects how courts evaluate government justifications","It sets court filing fees","It always guarantees a plaintiff wins","It replaces factual analysis"],0,"Standards of review affect the strength of the government's required justification and the fit between means and ends.","https://constitution.congress.gov/browse/amendment-14/"],["law102","Due process: notice and an opportunity to be heard","Procedural due process generally requires fair procedures before government deprives someone of protected life, liberty, or property interests. What process is due depends on context, including the private interest, risk of error, and governmental interest.","A state agency suspends a professional license without a prior hearing. Determine whether a protected interest exists, whether an emergency or later hearing changes the analysis, and what procedures apply.","Which is central to procedural due process?",["Fair procedures before or around qualifying government deprivations","The guarantee of winning an appeal","A right to any requested remedy","A duty only for private businesses"],0,"Notice and meaningful opportunities to be heard are core procedural protections subject to contextual rules.","https://constitution.congress.gov/browse/amendment-14/"],["law103","Reading statutes element by element","Statutory analysis begins with the enacted language and definitions. Identify verbs, exceptions, thresholds, cross-references, effective dates and judicial interpretations. Avoid treating legislative summaries as statutory text.","A statute provides a remedy only when a seller knowingly misrepresents a material fact. Separate knowledge, representation, materiality and remedies, then identify which facts support each.","What should you inspect when a statute contains an exception?",["Its text and any relevant definitions and cross-references","Only the statute's title","Only a news headline","The writer's preferences"],0,"Exceptions and defined terms can change the rule's scope.","https://uscode.house.gov/"],["law103","Checking case history and authority","Before relying on a case, verify the issuing court, year, procedural posture, later appellate treatment, and whether the statute has changed. Free research tools support partial verification but are not complete substitutes for professional citators.","A 1970 decision appears to support an argument. The relevant statute was rewritten in 2010. Explain why the older case may no longer be controlling and how you would investigate.","What is a warning that a cited opinion may not express current law?",["A later overruling decision or materially amended statute","A long case caption","A published reporter citation","A dissenting opinion alone"],0,"Subsequent history and statutory changes may alter the legal rule.","https://www.law.cornell.edu/"],["law104","Writing a persuasive but accurate argument","Advocacy writing advances a position while accurately characterizing the record and governing authority. Organize claims around disputed issues; distinguish cases that cut against you rather than concealing them.","Argue for a tenant seeking deposit return, then draft the landlord's strongest plausible response. Identify evidence you need and local statutes without inventing facts.","Which practice improves credible advocacy?",["Accurately address adverse authority","Invent supporting quotations","Omit material facts","Guarantee success"],0,"Honest treatment of adverse authority strengthens analysis and meets applicable professional responsibilities.","https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/legal_writing"],["law104","Facts, inferences and conclusions","Good legal writing separates observed facts, reasonable inferences and contested conclusions. A persuasive narrative never turns disputed allegations into established facts without evidence.","A witness says a driver looked at a phone before a crash. Distinguish the observation from the inference that the driver was distracted and the legal conclusion of negligence.","Which statement is an inference rather than an observed fact?",["The driver was distracted because they looked down","The witness reported looking down","The collision occurred at noon","A photograph shows rain"],0,"Distraction is inferred from an observation, and the inference must be tested against the evidence.","https://www.uscourts.gov/about-federal-courts/types-cases/civil-cases"],["law105","Evidence versus allegations","An accusation or pleading does not by itself prove an event. Evidence can include testimony, documents, photographs and other admissible materials, each subject to relevance, authenticity and procedural rules.","A renter says a landlord retained a deposit without cause. List the lease, payment records, dated photos and correspondence; distinguish each from the renter's conclusion about legality.","Why preserve dated photographs and communications?",["They may help establish disputed facts","They automatically win any case","They replace jurisdictional rules","They prove every witness is honest"],0,"Contemporaneous records can support factual claims, though admissibility and weight are context-dependent.","https://www.uscourts.gov/about-federal-courts/types-cases/civil-cases"],["law105","When a dispute may be resolved without trial","Negotiation, mediation, arbitration and judicial settlement can resolve disputes, but their legal effect differs. Arbitration clauses and settlements may affect rights, appeal options and enforcement; read the governing terms and law.","A service contract contains a mandatory arbitration clause. Identify the clause, potential statutory limits, costs and dispute procedures before assuming the consumer must or cannot arbitrate.","Why should someone read a proposed settlement agreement carefully?",["It can release claims and impose binding duties","It is always unenforceable","It guarantees a public court trial","It has no legal consequences"],0,"Settlement terms can create enforceable obligations and waive claims.","https://www.uscourts.gov/services-forms/alternative-dispute-resolution"],["law106","Competence and supervision","Professional competence requires appropriate knowledge, skill, preparation and thoroughness. A lawyer who lacks familiarity with a subject may need study, consultation or qualified assistance, within the governing ethical rules.","A lawyer with only family-law experience receives a complex securities matter. Analyze whether they can gain competence through preparation or collaboration and what supervision and communication duties apply.","What should a lawyer do when unfamiliar with an area of law?",["Evaluate competence and obtain appropriate preparation or assistance","Guarantee success regardless","Hide the gap from the client","Simply ignore deadlines"],0,"Competence can sometimes be developed with reasonable preparation or association, but duties remain.","https://www.americanbar.org/groups/professional_responsibility/publications/model_rules_of_professional_conduct/"],["law106","Handling client funds and avoiding misconduct","Rules governing safekeeping of client property generally require keeping client money separate from lawyer operating funds, accurate records and prompt accounting, subject to jurisdiction-specific trust-account requirements.","A lawyer receives settlement proceeds for a client. Identify the need for appropriate trust handling, accounting, resolution of disputed interests and delivery under applicable rules.","Why is commingling client funds with a firm's operating account generally prohibited?",["It risks misuse and violates duties to safeguard client property","All firms must hold cash only","Clients cannot receive settlements","It eliminates accounting duties"],0,"Professional conduct rules govern safekeeping, accounting and separation of client property.","https://www.americanbar.org/groups/professional_responsibility/publications/model_rules_of_professional_conduct/"]];
EXPANSION.forEach(([courseId,title,concept,hypo,question,options,answer,why,source],i)=>{const course=COURSES.find(c=>c.id===courseId);if(!course)return;course.lessons.push({id:courseId+"-expanded-"+(i+1),title,time:"35–45 min (guided target)",sections:[["Doctrine and governing principles",concept],["Apply the rule to a hypothetical",hypo],["Independent reading and verification","Research the authoritative source: "+source+" . Identify its jurisdiction, date, controlling status, and any exceptions. Then write a short IRAC response explaining the best argument on both sides. Source readings and written tasks are not automatically evaluated."]],quiz:{question,options,answer,why}})});

const app=document.querySelector("#app"),storeKey="law-academy-progress-v1";
let completed={};try{completed=JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(storeKey)||"{}")}catch(e){completed={}}
let active=null,choice=null,answered=false;
function save(){localStorage.setItem(storeKey,JSON.stringify(completed))}
const LEVELS=[["Pre-Law Foundations","Classical foundations","🏛"],["First-Year Law School (1L)","Core legal doctrine","⚖"],["Second-Year Law School (2L)","Evidence and procedure","▤"],["Third-Year Law School (3L)","Practice and advocacy","▥"],["Bar Exam Preparation","Skills and readiness","✎"],["Advanced Legal Practice","Complex legal problems","♜"]];
function header(){return '<header class="masthead"><div class="crest">⚖</div><div class="brandtitle">LAW ACADEMY</div><div class="motto">LEARN · ANALYZE · ARGUE · ADVOCATE · LEAD</div></header>'}
function nav(selected){const tabs=[["Home","⌂","home"],["Courses","▤","courses"],["Exams","☑","exams"],["Courtroom","⚖","courtroom"],["More","☷","more"]];return '<nav class="bottom-nav" aria-label="Main navigation">'+tabs.map(([name,ico,key])=>'<button class="navitem '+(selected===key?'on':'')+'" data-nav="'+key+'"><span>'+ico+'</span><small>'+name+'</small></button>').join('')+'</nav>'}
function bindNav(){document.querySelectorAll("[data-nav]").forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>screen(b.dataset.nav))}
function screen(name){if(name==="home")return renderHome();if(name==="courses")return renderCatalog();if(name==="exams")return renderExams();if(name==="courtroom")return renderCourtroom();return renderMore()}
function shell(contents,selected="home"){app.innerHTML='<main class="shell app-shell">'+contents+'</main>'+nav(selected);bindNav()}
function getProgress(){const total=COURSES[0].lessons.length;const done=COURSES[0].lessons.filter(x=>completed[x.id]).length;return {total,done,percent:Math.round(done/total*100)}}
function renderHome(){active=null;const p=getProgress();const next=COURSES[0].lessons.find(x=>!completed[x.id])||COURSES[0].lessons[0];const index=COURSES[0].lessons.indexOf(next);
shell('<div class="court-hero">'+header()+'<p class="hero-copy">Master the law. Build the skills<br>to think, argue, and lead.</p></div><section class="progress-panel"><div><span class="gold-label">CURRENT LEVEL</span><h2>Pre-Law Foundations</h2><p>Lesson '+(index+1)+' of '+p.total+'</p><div class="progress-row"><div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width:'+p.percent+'%"></div></div><span>'+p.percent+'%</span></div></div><div class="ring" style="--pc:'+p.percent+'%"><span>'+p.percent+'%</span><small>COURSE<br>MASTERY</small></div></section><div class="quick-grid">'+[["▤","Continue Learning","continue"],["☑","Practice Exams","exams"],["⚖","Virtual Courtroom","courtroom"],["◴","My Progress","progress"],["♧","Resources","resources"]].map(a=>'<button class="quick-card" data-action="'+a[2]+'"><span>'+a[0]+'</span><small>'+a[1]+'</small></button>').join('')+'</div><section class="feature-card"><span class="gold-label">FEATURED COURSE</span><h2>Introduction to the<br>American Legal System</h2><p>Discover how the courts, Constitution, and legal principles work.</p><button class="gold-button" id="resume"> '+(p.done?'Continue':'Start')+' Lesson '+(index+1)+' ›</button></section><div class="section"><h3>YOUR LAW PROGRAM</h3><button class="text-link" id="allCourses">View All Courses →</button></div><div class="level-list">'+LEVELS.map((l,i)=>'<button class="level-row" data-level="'+i+'"><span class="level-icon">'+l[2]+'</span><span class="level-text"><strong>Level '+(i+1)+' · '+l[0]+'</strong><small>'+(i===0?'6 courses · 5 lessons available':'Curriculum in development')+'</small></span><span class="level-arrow">'+(i===0?p.percent+'%':'›')+'</span></button>').join('')+'</div><p class="footer">Independent legal education · Not an accredited law school or legal advice.</p>','home');
document.querySelector("#resume").onclick=()=>renderLesson(COURSES[0],next);document.querySelector("#allCourses").onclick=renderCatalog;document.querySelectorAll("[data-action]").forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>{const a=b.dataset.action;if(a==="continue")renderLesson(COURSES[0],next);else if(a==="progress")renderProgress();else if(a==="resources")renderResources();else screen(a)});document.querySelectorAll("[data-level]").forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>{if(b.dataset.level==="0")renderCatalog();else renderPlanned(LEVELS[Number(b.dataset.level)][0])})}
const FUTURE_COURSES=[["Contracts","Torts","Civil Procedure","Criminal Law","Constitutional Law","Property","Legislation & Regulation","Legal Research & Writing"],["Evidence","Criminal Procedure","Business Associations","Administrative Law","Professional Responsibility","Federal Courts","Advanced Writing"],["Trial Advocacy","Appellate Advocacy","Negotiation","Clinical Practice","Advanced Research","Specializations"],["Bar Doctrine Review","Timed Multiple Choice","Essays","Performance Tests","Full Mock Exams"],["Complex Litigation","Corporate Transactions","Constitutional Litigation","Technology & Privacy","International Law","Capstone"]];
function renderCatalog(){const p=getProgress();
shell('<div class="pagehead"><span class="gold-label">YOUR LEARNING PATH</span><h1>Courses</h1><p>Start with Pre-Law Foundations. Future levels follow when you are ready.</p></div>'+
'<section class="current-level"><span class="gold-label">CURRENT LEVEL · LEVEL 1</span><h2>Pre-Law Foundations</h2><p>Build your knowledge from the ground up, one course at a time.</p><div class="progress-row"><div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width:'+p.percent+'%"></div></div><span>'+p.percent+'% of LAW 101</span></div></section>'+
'<div class="section"><h3>PRE-LAW COURSES</h3><span class="tag">6 courses</span></div><div class="level-list">'+COURSES.map((c,i)=>'<button class="level-row" data-course="'+c.id+'"><span class="level-icon">'+String(101+i)+'</span><span class="level-text"><strong>'+c.title+'</strong><small>'+(c.lessons.length?c.lessons.length+' lessons available · '+c.lessons.filter(l=>completed[l.id]).length+' mastered':'Planned · Lessons in development')+'</small></span><span class="level-arrow">›</span></button>').join('')+'</div>'+
'<div class="section"><h3>FUTURE ACADEMIC LEVELS</h3><span class="tag">Tap to expand</span></div><div class="future-levels">'+LEVELS.slice(1).map((l,i)=>'<details class="future-item"><summary><span class="level-icon">'+l[2]+'</span><span class="level-text"><strong>Level '+(i+2)+' · '+l[0]+'</strong><small>'+l[1]+' · Planned</small></span><span class="level-arrow">⌄</span></summary><div class="future-content"><p>Planned courses: '+FUTURE_COURSES[i].join(' · ')+' . Lessons and exams are not yet implemented.</p><button class="text-link" data-planned="'+(i+1)+'">View level details →</button></div></details>').join('')+'</div>','courses');
document.querySelectorAll("[data-course]").forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>renderCourse(COURSES.find(c=>c.id===b.dataset.course)));
document.querySelectorAll("[data-planned]").forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>renderPlanned(LEVELS[Number(b.dataset.planned)][0]));
}
function renderPlanned(title){shell('<div class="pagehead"><span class="gold-label">FUTURE CURRICULUM</span><h1>'+title+'</h1><p>This level is planned; lessons, examinations and simulations are not yet available. No placeholder content is counted as completed.</p></div><button class="gold-button" id="back">Browse available courses</button>','courses');document.querySelector("#back").onclick=renderCatalog}

const CASE_EXAM_BANK=[{"course":"law101","topic":"Court authority","facts":"A resident sues a neighbor over a fence in state court and argues that any disagreement involving constitutional rights must go directly to the U.S. Supreme Court.","question":"What is the most accurate response?","options":["The Supreme Court has mandatory original jurisdiction over every constitutional dispute","Trial courts ordinarily hear cases first; the proper court depends on jurisdiction and the claims","Every fence dispute belongs in federal district court","The plaintiff selects any court without restrictions"],"answer":1,"why":"Most disputes begin in trial courts; jurisdiction, pleadings and procedural rules determine where they belong.","id":"case-1"},{"course":"law101","topic":"Appeal posture","facts":"A plaintiff loses a jury trial and wants the appellate court to interview every witness again.","question":"What does an appellate court ordinarily do?","options":["Review claimed legal or procedural errors under applicable standards","Always hold a brand-new trial","Write a new statute to reverse the verdict","Investigate events without regard to the record"],"answer":0,"why":"Appeals typically review the lower court's record for alleged error, rather than automatically retrying factual disputes.","id":"case-2"},{"course":"law102","topic":"Public speech","facts":"A city official denies a peaceful rally permit because they dislike its political message.","question":"Which fact most directly raises First Amendment concern?","options":["Government action based on viewpoint","The presence of a public park alone","The number of police officers","The presence of a written application"],"answer":0,"why":"Viewpoint discrimination by government is a significant constitutional concern; forum and applicable rules still matter.","id":"case-3"},{"course":"law102","topic":"Administrative license","facts":"A state suspends a professional license without notice; no immediate public danger is described.","question":"What should an analyst assess first?","options":["Whether a protected interest exists and what procedures are due","Whether the person likes the agency","Whether every suspension is criminal","Whether the applicant paid federal tax"],"answer":0,"why":"Procedural due process can require notice and a meaningful opportunity to be heard, with context-specific qualifications.","id":"case-4"},{"course":"law103","topic":"Case citation","facts":"A blog cites an appellate opinion supporting a rule, but the controlling jurisdiction issued a later contrary decision.","question":"What is the best research approach?","options":["Use the blog because it is simpler","Check the hierarchy, dates, subsequent history and binding local authority","Cite only the older case","Treat both opinions as statutes"],"answer":1,"why":"Binding authority and subsequent history determine how a court should treat the rule.","id":"case-5"},{"course":"law103","topic":"Statutory definitions","facts":"A consumer statute covers 'covered merchants' as defined in another section; a student's answer ignores that definition.","question":"What should the student do?","options":["Read the definition and cross-references before applying the rule","Assume the term includes every business","Rely only on the statute title","Ignore the jurisdiction"],"answer":0,"why":"Defined terms and cross-references control the scope of statutory provisions.","id":"case-6"},{"course":"law104","topic":"Objective memo","facts":"A memo discusses only facts favorable to the client and ignores a directly adverse precedent.","question":"Which revision is most appropriate?","options":["Remove all citations","Address the adverse authority and distinguish it where defensible","Guarantee a win","Rewrite the precedent's holding"],"answer":1,"why":"Accurate objective analysis includes counterarguments and controlling adverse authority.","id":"case-7"},{"course":"law104","topic":"IRAC analysis","facts":"A shop employee saw a spill 20 minutes before a customer slipped. The student's memo simply says 'negligence is obvious.'","question":"Which improvement matters most?","options":["Apply duty, breach, causation and harm to evidence, with defenses and unknown jurisdiction","Add more adjectives","Omit the injury","Declare criminal liability"],"answer":0,"why":"Legal analysis tests each element against relevant facts and opposing evidence.","id":"case-8"},{"course":"law105","topic":"Consumer claim","facts":"A customer wants a refund after a disputed online purchase but has not identified the seller's terms or governing state.","question":"What should be gathered first?","options":["Terms, communications, dates and applicable jurisdiction","Only a social media opinion","A criminal jury verdict","An unrelated federal opinion"],"answer":0,"why":"Contracts and consumer protections often turn on documented terms, facts and jurisdiction.","id":"case-9"},{"course":"law105","topic":"Settlement","facts":"Two parties settle a dispute and sign an agreement releasing specified claims.","question":"Why read the release carefully?","options":["It may create binding obligations and waive claims","It is only ceremonial","It always guarantees an appeal","It necessarily proves criminal guilt"],"answer":0,"why":"A settlement may affect claims and obligations, depending on its terms and governing law.","id":"case-10"},{"course":"law106","topic":"Conflict of interest","facts":"An attorney is asked to represent two directly opposing parties in the same dispute.","question":"What is the proper starting point?","options":["Evaluate the conflict under applicable professional rules, including whether consent is legally possible","Assume a verbal waiver always works","Ignore the issue until trial","Ask both sides to conceal it"],"answer":0,"why":"Direct adversity triggers serious conflict rules; some conflicts are nonconsentable.","id":"case-11"},{"course":"law106","topic":"Client funds","facts":"A lawyer deposits client settlement funds into a personal operating account without tracking them.","question":"Which duty is most directly implicated?","options":["Safeguarding and separating client property","Jury selection","Federal jurisdiction","Pleading standards"],"answer":0,"why":"Jurisdictional professional rules generally require appropriate handling and accounting of client property.","id":"case-12"}];
const EXAM_KEY="law-academy-exam-results-v1";
let examResults={};try{examResults=JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(EXAM_KEY)||"{}")||{}}catch(e){examResults={}}
const CAPSTONE_KEY="law-academy-capstone-v1";
const CAPSTONE=[
{question:"A city denies a public-park permit explicitly because officials dislike the group's message. What is the strongest initial legal concern?",options:["Viewpoint discrimination by government","A private contract dispute","Federal diversity jurisdiction","A mandatory jury trial"],answer:0,why:"An official distinction based on viewpoint raises serious First Amendment concerns; forum and applicable law must still be analyzed."},
{question:"A federal appellate opinion from another circuit addresses a similar federal issue. How should it normally be treated?",options:["Automatically binding everywhere","Potentially persuasive, not automatically controlling","As a federal statute","As irrelevant"],answer:1,why:"A different circuit's opinion ordinarily provides persuasive authority, while Supreme Court and controlling circuit precedents govern."},
{question:"A state statute is allegedly inconsistent with a valid federal statute. What inquiry matters?",options:["Which text is shorter","Whether federal law preempts the state rule","Which governor is in office","Whether the issue is popular"],answer:1,why:"Preemption depends on valid federal authority and express, field or conflict preemption analysis."},
{question:"A customer slips after a worker noticed a spill 20 minutes earlier. Which missing issue must still be considered?",options:["Whether the store's sign is attractive","Causation, injury, and applicable duty standards","Whether the shopper owns the store","Whether criminal intent is proven"],answer:1,why:"Notice may matter to breach, but causation, injury and governing state negligence standards are still necessary."},
{question:"In Marbury v. Madison, why was the requested writ not issued?",options:["The Court concluded it lacked the relevant original jurisdiction","Marbury withdrew the case","Congress abolished all judicial review","The President confessed"],answer:0,why:"The Court concluded the statutory original-jurisdiction authorization was inconsistent with Article III."},
{question:"An attorney receives confidential client information. Which statement is best?",options:["All information is automatically public","Professional confidentiality and evidentiary privilege are distinct","Confidentiality never has exceptions","The client cannot discuss their own case"],answer:1,why:"Ethical confidentiality and attorney-client privilege differ in coverage and exceptions."},
{question:"Which source is ordinarily primary law?",options:["An unsigned blog summary","An enacted statute","A general newspaper article","A student study guide"],answer:1,why:"Statutes, judicial decisions, constitutions and valid regulations are primary legal authorities."},
{question:"Which legal memorandum technique is strongest?",options:["Address only favorable facts","Analyze controlling law, counterarguments and uncertainty","Guarantee the client's desired result","Use uncited quotations"],answer:1,why:"An objective memorandum assesses both sides and identifies unresolved law and facts."},
{question:"In a criminal trial, the prosecution must ordinarily prove guilt by which standard?",options:["Preponderance","Reasonable possibility","Beyond a reasonable doubt","Any suspicion"],answer:2,why:"The government must prove each element of the charged crime beyond a reasonable doubt."},
{question:"A lawyer finds a fabricated opinion in a draft. What should they do?",options:["File it unchanged","Verify and replace or remove it, meeting candor obligations","Invent a reporter citation","Hide the reference"],answer:1,why:"Attorneys must verify authorities and meet applicable professional duties of candor."},
{question:"Which IRAC component tests legal elements against particular facts?",options:["Issue","Rule","Application","Conclusion"],answer:2,why:"Application explains how law applies to evidence and competing arguments."},
{question:"A landlord-deposit dispute arises in an unnamed state. What is the best research starting point?",options:["Apply one universal rule","Identify the jurisdiction and controlling statute","Assume all leases are the same","Rely only on online comments"],answer:1,why:"Housing rules and deadlines are jurisdiction-specific."}
];
function renderCapstone(){let index=0;const answers=[];function question(){const q=CAPSTONE[index];shell('<div class="pagehead"><span class="gold-label">LEVEL 1 · CUMULATIVE PRACTICE</span><h1>Foundations Exam</h1><p>Question '+(index+1)+' of '+CAPSTONE.length+' · 80% passing threshold</p></div><div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width:'+(index/CAPSTONE.length*100)+'%"></div></div><div class="question exam-question"><h2>'+q.question+'</h2>'+q.options.map((o,i)=>'<button class="option" data-cap="'+i+'">'+String.fromCharCode(65+i)+'. '+o+'</button>').join('')+'<button class="gold-button" id="capNext" disabled>'+(index===CAPSTONE.length-1?'Grade exam':'Next question')+'</button></div><button class="back" id="capExit">← Exams</button>','exams');document.querySelector("#capExit").onclick=renderExams;document.querySelectorAll("[data-cap]").forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>{answers[index]=Number(b.dataset.cap);document.querySelectorAll("[data-cap]").forEach(x=>x.classList.toggle("selected",b===x));document.querySelector("#capNext").disabled=false});document.querySelector("#capNext").onclick=()=>{if(answers[index]===undefined)return;index++;if(index<CAPSTONE.length)question();else result()}}function result(){const correct=CAPSTONE.filter((q,i)=>q.answer===answers[i]).length,score=Math.round(100*correct/CAPSTONE.length);let old={};try{old=JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(CAPSTONE_KEY)||"{}")}catch(e){}localStorage.setItem(CAPSTONE_KEY,JSON.stringify({last:score,best:Math.max(old.best||0,score),attempts:(old.attempts||0)+1}));shell('<div class="pagehead"><span class="gold-label">LEVEL 1 · PRACTICE EXAM RESULTS</span><h1>'+score+'% · '+(score>=80?'Practice pass':'Review needed')+'</h1><p>'+correct+' of '+CAPSTONE.length+' correct</p></div><div class="review-list">'+CAPSTONE.map((q,i)=>'<div class="review-item"><span class="gold-label">'+(answers[i]===q.answer?'✓ CORRECT':'REVIEW')+'</span><h3>'+q.question+'</h3><p><strong>Selected:</strong> '+q.options[answers[i]]+'</p><p><strong>Correct:</strong> '+q.options[q.answer]+'</p><p>'+q.why+'</p></div>').join('')+'</div><button class="gold-button" id="capRetry">Retake exam</button><button class="button secondary" id="capClose">Return to exams</button><p class="footer">This practice score is not a credential, bar-readiness assessment, or verified Level 1 graduation.</p>','exams');document.querySelector("#capRetry").onclick=renderCapstone;document.querySelector("#capClose").onclick=renderExams}question()}
function renderExams(){
const available=COURSES.filter(c=>c.lessons.length>0);
shell('<div class="pagehead"><span class="gold-label">ASSESSMENT CENTER</span><h1>Practice Exams</h1><p>Practice across multiple lessons. Grades are saved separately from lesson completion.</p></div>'+
'<div class="feature-card compact"><span class="gold-label">LEVEL 1 · FOUNDATION ASSESSMENTS</span><h2>Course Practice Exams</h2><p>Each exam uses all currently available lesson questions for that course. 80% is required to pass; review every answer afterward.</p><button class="gold-button" id="socratic">Guided Professor Exercise ›</button></div>'+
'<button class="level-row" id="capstoneLaunch"><span class="level-icon">★</span><span class="level-text"><strong>Level 1 Cumulative Practice Exam</strong><small>12 original questions · all six subjects</small></span><span class="level-arrow">›</span></button><div class="level-list">'+available.map(c=>'<button class="level-row" data-exam="'+c.id+'"><span class="level-icon">☑</span><span class="level-text"><strong>'+c.title+'</strong><small>'+(c.lessons.length+CASE_EXAM_BANK.filter(x=>x.course===c.id).length)+' questions · '+(examResults[c.id]?'Best score '+examResults[c.id].best+'%':'Not attempted')+'</small></span><span class="level-arrow">›</span></button>').join('')+'</div>'+
'<p class="footer">These are formative exams based on the current introductory lesson bank, not university-equivalent midterms or bar exams. Additional original questions and written assessments are planned.</p>','exams');
document.querySelector("#socratic").onclick=renderProfessor;document.querySelector("#capstoneLaunch").onclick=renderCapstone;
document.querySelectorAll("[data-exam]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>runExam(COURSES.find(c=>c.id===x.dataset.exam)));
}
function runExam(course){let pos=0,answers=[],submitted=false;const questions=course.lessons.map(l=>({...l.quiz,lesson:l.title})).concat(CASE_EXAM_BANK.filter(x=>x.course===course.id).map(x=>({question:x.facts+' '+x.question,options:x.options,answer:x.answer,why:x.why,lesson:'Case analysis: '+x.topic})));function draw(){
const q=questions[pos];shell('<div class="pagehead"><span class="gold-label">PRACTICE EXAM · '+course.id.toUpperCase()+'</span><h1>Question '+(pos+1)+' of '+questions.length+'</h1><p>'+course.title+'</p></div><div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width:'+Math.round((pos/questions.length)*100)+'%"></div></div><div class="question exam-question"><span class="tag">TOPIC · '+q.lesson+'</span><h2>'+q.question+'</h2>'+q.options.map((o,i)=>'<button class="option" data-exam-choice="'+i+'">'+String.fromCharCode(65+i)+'. '+o+'</button>').join('')+'<button class="button" id="nextQuestion" disabled>'+(pos===questions.length-1?'Grade exam':'Next question')+'</button></div><button class="back" id="cancelExam">← Leave practice exam</button>','exams');
document.querySelector("#cancelExam").onclick=renderExams;
document.querySelectorAll("[data-exam-choice]").forEach(btn=>btn.onclick=()=>{if(submitted)return;answers[pos]=Number(btn.dataset.examChoice);document.querySelectorAll("[data-exam-choice]").forEach(b=>b.classList.toggle("selected",b===btn));document.querySelector("#nextQuestion").disabled=false});
document.querySelector("#nextQuestion").onclick=()=>{if(answers[pos]===undefined)return;pos++;if(pos<questions.length)draw();else results()};}
function results(){const correct=questions.reduce((n,q,i)=>n+(answers[i]===q.answer?1:0),0),score=Math.round(100*correct/questions.length),old=examResults[course.id];examResults[course.id]={best:Math.max(old?old.best:0,score),last:score,attempts:(old?old.attempts:0)+1};localStorage.setItem(EXAM_KEY,JSON.stringify(examResults));
shell('<div class="pagehead"><span class="gold-label">PRACTICE EXAM RESULTS</span><h1>'+score+'% · '+(score>=80?'Passed':'Needs practice')+'</h1><p>'+correct+' of '+questions.length+' correct · '+course.title+'</p></div><div class="feature-card compact"><h2>Review your reasoning</h2><p>Read each explanation and return to any lesson you want to study again.</p></div><div class="review-list">'+questions.map((q,i)=>'<div class="review-item"><span class="gold-label">'+(answers[i]===q.answer?'✓ CORRECT':'REVIEW NEEDED')+' · '+q.lesson+'</span><h3>'+q.question+'</h3><p><strong>Your answer:</strong> '+q.options[answers[i]]+'</p><p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> '+q.options[q.answer]+'</p><p>'+q.why+'</p></div>').join('')+'</div><button class="gold-button" id="retakeExam">Try again</button><button class="button secondary" id="exitExam">Back to exams</button>','exams');document.querySelector("#retakeExam").onclick=()=>runExam(course);document.querySelector("#exitExam").onclick=renderExams;}draw()}
function renderProfessor(){
const steps=[
{question:"A city bans all protests because officials dislike the protestors' opinions. What is the first issue to identify?",options:["Whether the city is a government actor and whether expression is protected","Whether protestors own the streets","Whether the mayor is popular"],correct:0,explain:"Start with state action and the constitutional protection at issue. A city is a government actor, and viewpoint-based restrictions raise serious First Amendment concerns."},
{question:"What additional facts should you request before reaching a confident legal conclusion?",options:["None; every permit rule is invalid","The location, rule text, stated reason, enforcement history, and applicable precedent","Only whether the protestors won an election"],correct:1,explain:"You must examine the actual rule, forum, justification, facts, and controlling law. Avoid treating a broad principle as a complete legal test."},
{question:"Which answer shows the strongest legal reasoning?",options:["The city always wins because it owns the park","The protestors always win, without considering the facts","A viewpoint-based ban likely faces constitutional problems, but precise analysis requires the ordinance, setting, and controlling doctrine"],correct:2,explain:"The best answer identifies the relevant principle while stating what still needs verification. This is a guided exercise, not an individualized opinion."}
,
{question:"The city claims it now requires permits for every gathering above 200 people. What should a court examine?",options:["Whether a facially neutral permit rule is automatically valid","Whether the rule meets applicable public-forum standards and is administered without viewpoint discrimination","Whether the crowd number is even"],correct:1,explain:"Facially neutral restrictions can still raise constitutional problems. Examine the forum, tailoring, governmental interest, administrative discretion, and alternatives."},
{question:"Officials waived the rule for a favored political group. Why does that matter?",options:["Selective enforcement can be evidence of viewpoint discrimination","Political groups are never covered by permits","Any waiver makes a rule invalid in every case"],correct:0,explain:"Inconsistent enforcement may suggest the real distinction is viewpoint rather than neutral crowd-management considerations."},
{question:"What should your written conclusion include?",options:["A guaranteed victory for one side","Only the most favorable facts","A qualified conclusion with controlling authority, contrary arguments, and unresolved facts"],correct:2,explain:"A defensible legal conclusion evaluates evidence and competing arguments, while identifying the rules and facts still needing verification."}
];let step=0;
function draw(){const x=steps[step];shell('<div class="pagehead"><span class="gold-label">GUIDED PROFESSOR · RULE-BASED</span><h1>Socratic Practice</h1><p>Public protest hypothetical · Step '+(step+1)+' of '+steps.length+'</p></div><div class="question"><p class="tag">YOUR PROFESSOR ASKS</p><h2>'+x.question+'</h2>'+x.options.map((o,i)=>'<button class="option" data-answer="'+i+'">'+o+'</button>').join('')+'<div id="profFeedback"></div><button id="profNext" class="gold-button" style="display:none">'+(step===steps.length-1?'Finish exercise':'Next question')+'</button></div><button class="back" id="profExit">← Back to exams</button>','exams');document.querySelector("#profExit").onclick=renderExams;document.querySelectorAll("[data-answer]").forEach(btn=>btn.onclick=()=>{const selected=Number(btn.dataset.answer);document.querySelectorAll("[data-answer]").forEach(b=>b.disabled=true);document.querySelector("#profFeedback").innerHTML='<div class="feedback"><strong>'+(selected===x.correct?'Good reasoning.':'Consider this correction.')+'</strong><p>'+x.explain+'</p></div>';document.querySelector("#profNext").style.display="block"});document.querySelector("#profNext").onclick=()=>{step++;if(step<steps.length)draw();else renderExams()}}draw()}
function renderCourtroom(){shell('<div class="pagehead"><span class="gold-label">PRACTICAL SKILLS</span><h1>Virtual Courtroom</h1><p>Practice arguing both sides of a dispute, examining evidence, and defending a legal position.</p></div><div class="feature-card compact"><span class="gold-label">COMING IN A FUTURE BUILD</span><h2>Trial & Appellate Simulations</h2><p>Interactive courtroom cases are planned but not yet playable. You can begin building your reasoning skills with LAW 101 now.</p><button class="gold-button" id="go">Study Legal Reasoning ›</button></div>','courtroom');document.querySelector("#go").onclick=()=>renderLesson(COURSES[0],COURSES[0].lessons[4])}
function renderProgress(){const all=COURSES.flatMap(c=>c.lessons),done=all.filter(l=>completed[l.id]).length,total=all.length;let cap={};try{cap=JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(CAPSTONE_KEY)||"{}")}catch(e){}const passed=COURSES.filter(c=>examResults[c.id]&&examResults[c.id].best>=80);const drafts=(typeof writingDrafts==="object"?writingDrafts:{});const drafted=(typeof WRITING_TASKS==="object"?WRITING_TASKS.filter(t=>(drafts[t.id]||"").trim().length>0).length:0);
shell('<div class="pagehead"><span class="gold-label">LEVEL 1 · ACADEMIC PROGRESS</span><h1>My Progress</h1><p>Lesson quizzes, practice exams, and written assignments are separate measures.</p></div><section class="progress-panel"><div><span class="gold-label">FOUNDATION LESSONS</span><h2>'+done+' / '+total+' completed</h2><p>Passing each short lesson check does not establish full course mastery.</p><div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width:'+(total?Math.round(done/total*100):0)+'%"></div></div></div><div class="ring" style="--pc:'+(total?Math.round(done/total*100):0)+'%"><span>'+(total?Math.round(done/total*100):0)+'%</span></div></section><div class="section"><h3>ACADEMIC CHECKPOINTS</h3></div><div class="level-list">'+[
['Lessons passed',done+' / '+total],
['Course practice exams ≥80%',passed.length+' / '+COURSES.length],
['Cumulative exam ≥80%',cap.best>=80?'Practice pass recorded':'Not yet passed'],
['Writing drafts started',drafted+' / '+WRITING_TASKS.length],
['Evaluated writing and final oral defense','Not yet available']
].map(x=>'<div class="level-row"><span class="level-icon">▤</span><span class="level-text"><strong>'+x[0]+'</strong><small>'+x[1]+'</small></span></div>').join('')+'</div><div class="review-item"><h3>Level 1 graduation</h3><p>Not available yet. Graduation will require verified mastery across coursework, exams, substantive written work, research, and a final legal-reasoning assessment. Practice scores and word checks cannot substitute for evaluated legal analysis.</p></div>','more')}
function renderResources(){shell('<div class="pagehead"><span class="gold-label">REFERENCE CENTER</span><h1>Legal Library</h1><p>Start with authoritative public legal resources. Verify laws and current decisions for your jurisdiction.</p></div><div class="level-list"><a class="level-row" href="https://opencasebook.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span class="level-icon">▤</span><span class="level-text"><strong>Harvard H2O Open Casebook</strong><small>Free open casebooks · Check reuse conditions</small></span><span class="level-arrow">↗</span></a><a class="level-row" href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span class="level-icon">⚖</span><span class="level-text"><strong>Cornell Legal Information Institute</strong><small>Legal definitions, statutes, and court opinions</small></span><span class="level-arrow">↗</span></a><a class="level-row" href="https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/opinions.aspx" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span class="level-icon">▤</span><span class="level-text"><strong>U.S. Supreme Court Opinions</strong><small>Official published opinions</small></span><span class="level-arrow">↗</span></a><a class="level-row" href="https://constitution.congress.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span class="level-icon">§</span><span class="level-text"><strong>Constitution Annotated</strong><small>Congressional constitutional analysis</small></span><span class="level-arrow">↗</span></a></div>','more')}
function renderMore(){shell('<div class="pagehead"><span class="gold-label">YOUR ACADEMY</span><h1>More</h1><p>Learning tools and settings</p></div><div class="level-list"><button class="level-row" id="progress"><span class="level-icon">◴</span><span class="level-text"><strong>My Progress</strong><small>Mastery and completed lessons</small></span><span class="level-arrow">›</span></button><button class="level-row" id="writing"><span class="level-icon">✎</span><span class="level-text"><strong>Case Studies & Writing</strong><small>Real opinions, IRAC, and saved drafts</small></span><span class="level-arrow">›</span></button><button class="level-row" id="resources"><span class="level-icon">♧</span><span class="level-text"><strong>Legal Library</strong><small>Official and educational resources</small></span><span class="level-arrow">›</span></button><button class="level-row" id="settings"><span class="level-icon">⚙</span><span class="level-text"><strong>Settings</strong><small>Independent study · Device-local progress</small></span><span class="level-arrow">›</span></button></div>','more');document.querySelector("#progress").onclick=renderProgress;document.querySelector("#resources").onclick=renderResources;document.querySelector("#writing").onclick=writingHome;document.querySelector("#settings").onclick=()=>{shell('<div class="pagehead"><h1>Settings</h1><p>Law Academy stores mastery progress in this browser. Clearing browser site data may erase it.</p></div><div class="feature-card compact"><h2>About Law Academy</h2><p>Independent legal education from foundational courses toward advanced legal practice. This application is not an accredited school and does not provide legal advice.</p></div>','more')}}
function renderCourse(c){app.innerHTML='<main class="shell">'+header()+'<button class="back" id="back">← All courses</button><p class="eyebrow">'+c.level+'</p><h2>'+c.title+'</h2><p class="muted">'+(c.lessons.length?'Read each lesson and pass its mastery check to mark it complete.':'This course is planned but has not yet been written. No placeholder content is counted as completed.')+'</p>'+c.lessons.map((l,i)=>'<button class="lesson" data-lesson="'+i+'"><span class="tag">Lesson '+(i+1)+(completed[l.id]?' · ✓ Mastered':'')+'</span><strong style="display:block;margin-top:8px">'+l.title+'</strong><small>'+l.time+' · lesson + question</small></button>').join('')+'<button class="button secondary" id="home">Return to dashboard</button></main>';document.querySelector("#back").onclick=renderHome;document.querySelector("#home").onclick=renderHome;document.querySelectorAll("[data-lesson]").forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>renderLesson(c,c.lessons[Number(b.dataset.lesson)]))}
function renderLesson(c,l){active=l;choice=null;answered=false;app.innerHTML='<main class="shell">'+header()+'<button class="back" id="back">← '+c.title+'</button><p class="eyebrow">'+c.level+' · '+l.time+'</p><h2>'+l.title+'</h2><article class="reading">'+l.sections.map(([h,p],i)=>'<h3>'+h+'</h3><p class="'+(i===l.sections.length-1?'example':'')+'">'+p+'</p>').join('')+'</article><div class="section"><h3>Mastery check</h3><span class="tag">1 question</span></div><div class="question"><strong>'+l.quiz.question+'</strong><div id="choices">'+l.quiz.options.map((o,i)=>'<button class="option" data-choice="'+i+'">'+String.fromCharCode(65+i)+'. '+o+'</button>').join('')+'</div><div id="feedback"></div><button class="button" id="submit" disabled>Check answer</button></div><p class="footer">Study guidance only; not legal advice. Check authoritative current law for real matters.</p></main>';document.querySelector("#back").onclick=()=>renderCourse(c);document.querySelectorAll("[data-choice]").forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>{if(answered)return;choice=Number(b.dataset.choice);document.querySelectorAll(".option").forEach(x=>x.classList.toggle("selected",x===b));document.querySelector("#submit").disabled=false});document.querySelector("#submit").onclick=()=>{if(choice===null||answered)return;answered=true;let ok=choice===l.quiz.answer;if(ok){completed[l.id]=true;save()}document.querySelector("#feedback").innerHTML='<div class="feedback"><strong>'+(ok?'Correct — lesson mastered':'Not quite — review the explanation')+'</strong><p>'+l.quiz.why+'</p></div>';let b=document.querySelector("#submit");b.textContent=ok?'Back to course':'Try again';b.disabled=false;b.onclick=()=>{if(ok)renderCourse(c);else renderLesson(c,l)}}}
const WRITING_KEY="law-academy-writing-v1";
const WRITING_TASKS=[
{id:"marbury",title:"Case Brief: Marbury v. Madison",course:"LAW 102",source:"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/5/137/",background:"Read the 1803 opinion. William Marbury sought a writ of mandamus after his judicial commission was not delivered.",prompt:"Write the facts, procedural posture, issue, rule, holding, reasoning, and explain why the Court denied the requested writ.",checks:["Material facts","Procedural posture","Article III jurisdiction issue","Holding and reasoning","Judicial review analysis"]},
{id:"negligence",title:"IRAC: Store Slip-and-Fall",course:"LAW 104",source:"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/negligence",background:"Hypothetical: An employee sees a drink spilled in an aisle at 2:00 p.m. No warning is placed. A customer slips at 2:20 p.m. and is injured. Jurisdiction is unspecified.",prompt:"Use IRAC to examine duty, breach, causation, damages, counterarguments, and missing jurisdiction-specific facts.",checks:["Precise issue","Applicable negligence elements","Application to facts","Opposing arguments","Missing facts and limitations"]},
{id:"speech",title:"Constitutional Analysis: Public Demonstration",course:"LAW 102 / LAW 104",source:"https://constitution.congress.gov/browse/amendment-1/",background:"Fictional scenario: A town requires permits for demonstrations of more than 100 people in a public park. Officials deny a permit after publicly criticizing the applicants viewpoints, while approving a similarly sized event supporting the town government. There is no evidence in the prompt about crowd safety or appeals procedures.",prompt:"Draft an objective legal memorandum using Issue, Rule, Application, Conclusion. Analyze government action, the public-forum setting, viewpoint discrimination, possible content-neutral justifications, selective enforcement, and the facts or binding cases needed before a definitive conclusion. Argue for both the town and the applicants.",checks:["Precisely frame the First Amendment question","Explain state action and forum analysis","Discuss viewpoint neutrality and selective enforcement","Explain opposing arguments and factual limits","Write a cautious evidence-based conclusion"]},
{id:"research-portfolio",title:"Legal Research Portfolio: Source Verification",course:"LAW 103",source:"https://www.law.cornell.edu/",background:"Research scenario: An online article claims every U.S. landlord must return a security deposit in 14 days, and that every court applies the same rule. Your task is to test those claims rather than assume they are correct.",prompt:"Choose one U.S. state and identify its official governing statute and any material exceptions or deadlines. Record the jurisdiction, statute title and section, official source URL, date checked, relevant language in your own words, one credible secondary explanation, and the limits of your research. Explain why a national blanket rule cannot be accepted without verification. Do not provide advice about any real person's dispute.",checks:["Chosen jurisdiction and legal issue clearly stated","Official statute identified with section and link","Primary versus secondary source correctly separated","Date checked and possible amendments considered","Limits, exceptions, and uncertainty explained"]}
];
let writingDrafts={};try{writingDrafts=JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(WRITING_KEY)||"{}")||{}}catch(e){writingDrafts={}}
function writingHome(){shell('<div class="pagehead"><span class="gold-label">PRE-LAW · WRITING LAB</span><h1>Case Studies & Writing</h1><p>Practice legal analysis and save drafts on this device. Exercises are ungraded.</p></div><div class="level-list">'+WRITING_TASKS.map(t=>'<button class="level-row" data-write="'+t.id+'"><span class="level-icon">✎</span><span class="level-text"><strong>'+t.title+'</strong><small>'+t.course+' · '+(writingDrafts[t.id]?'Draft saved':'Not started')+'</small></span><span class="level-arrow">›</span></button>').join('')+'</div>','more');document.querySelectorAll("[data-write]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>writingTask(WRITING_TASKS.find(t=>t.id===x.dataset.write)))}
function analyzeDraft(t,value){
const str=value.trim(),words=str?str.split(/\s+/).length:0;
const concepts=t.id==="marbury"?[
["Facts and posture",/marbury|commission|appointment|mandamus|delivery/i,"Identify the commission dispute, who sought relief, and how the case reached the Supreme Court."],
["Jurisdiction",/jurisdiction|article\s*(iii|3)|original/i,"Explain why the Court's original jurisdiction was central to the outcome."],
["Judicial review",/judicial\s*review|unconstitutional|constitution|invalid/i,"Address the conflict between the statute and the Constitution, using the actual opinion."],
["Holding and reasoning",/holding|held|reason|court\s*(denied|concluded|found)/i,"Separate the Court's disposition from its wider reasoning."]
]:[
["Issue and rule",/issue|rule|negligen|duty/i,"Frame a precise negligence issue and identify the governing elements."],
["Breach",/breach|spill|notice|warning|clean/i,"Apply the employee's notice and 20-minute delay to the standard of care."],
["Causation and harm",/caus|injur|damage|harm/i,"Analyze factual causation, legal causation, and the documented injury separately."],
["Counterarguments",/however|although|counter|defen|comparative|unknown|jurisdiction/i,"Address a plausible defense and identify the missing jurisdiction-specific law."]
];
const missing=concepts.filter(x=>!x[1].test(str));
const items=[];
if(words<120)items.push("Your draft is short for this exercise ("+words+" words). Develop the rule and its application with specific evidence.");
if(!/because|therefore|since|given|however|although|while/i.test(str))items.push("Explain why the facts support your conclusion; connect claims with reasoning, not only labels.");
for(const m of missing)items.push(m[2]);
if(!items.length)items.push("Your draft mentions the major checkpoints. Verify each proposition against the source; keyword checks cannot establish legal accuracy.");
return {words,covered:concepts.length-missing.length,total:concepts.length,items};
}
function writingTask(t){shell('<div class="pagehead"><span class="gold-label">'+t.course+' · WRITING ASSIGNMENT</span><h1>'+t.title+'</h1><p>'+t.background+'</p></div><a class="level-row" href="'+t.source+'" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span class="level-icon">▤</span><span class="level-text"><strong>Read the source</strong><small>External legal reference</small></span><span class="level-arrow">↗</span></a><div class="review-item"><h3>Your assignment</h3><p>'+t.prompt+'</p></div><label class="gold-label" for="writingDraft">YOUR DRAFT</label><textarea id="writingDraft" class="writing-field" rows="13" placeholder="Write your analysis here..."></textarea><button id="saveDraft" class="gold-button">Save draft</button><button id="reviewDraft" class="button secondary" type="button">Get guided feedback</button><div id="guidedFeedback" aria-live="polite"></div><span id="draftStatus" class="muted" aria-live="polite"></span><div class="review-item"><h3>Self-review checklist</h3>'+t.checks.map(c=>'<label class="check-line"><input type="checkbox"> '+c+'</label>').join('')+'<p>Checklist completion does not constitute an assessed grade.</p></div><button id="writingBack" class="button secondary">Back to assignments</button>','more');const field=document.querySelector("#writingDraft");field.value=writingDrafts[t.id]||"";const save=()=>{writingDrafts[t.id]=field.value;localStorage.setItem(WRITING_KEY,JSON.stringify(writingDrafts));document.querySelector("#draftStatus").textContent="Saved on this device"};document.querySelector("#saveDraft").onclick=save;document.querySelector("#reviewDraft").onclick=()=>{save();const a=analyzeDraft(t,field.value);const escapeText=x=>x.replace(/&/g,"&amp;").replace(/</g,"&lt;").replace(/>/g,"&gt;");document.querySelector("#guidedFeedback").innerHTML='<div class="review-item"><span class="gold-label">STRUCTURED WRITING FEEDBACK · NOT A GRADE</span><h3>Revision guidance</h3><p>'+a.words+' words · '+a.covered+' of '+a.total+' topic indicators detected</p><ul class="feedback-points">'+a.items.map(x=>"<li>"+escapeText(x)+"</li>").join("")+'</ul><p>These checks look for terms and structure, not whether your legal arguments are true. Verify the primary source, compare both sides, and revise your draft.</p></div>';document.querySelector("#guidedFeedback").scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth",block:"nearest"})};document.querySelector("#writingBack").onclick=()=>{save();writingHome()}}
renderHome();
